  "attributionNote": "Yoga Sutras, attributed to Patañjali, as Heritage IITKanpur's Scripture Server catalogues it (YGS), on its own shelf.",
  "description": "The Yoga Sūtras, compiled by Patañjali in Sanskrit, are some 195 terse aphorisms in four chapters (pādas) that found the Yoga school of Indian philosophy. Their subject is the mind: the second sūtra defines yoga as the stilling of its movements, and the rest sets out the obstacles, the eight limbs of practice, the powers that arise on the way, and freedom (kaivalya), which is the text's purpose. The sūtras assume the Sāṃkhya analysis of spirit and nature, and are so compressed that they have always been read with a commentary, first the Yoga-bhāṣya ascribed to Vyāsa. They are for the practitioner willing to train attention, and are best read slowly, a few at a time. The summary above is the one Heritage IITKanpur's Scripture Server publishes for the text, and the server's own metadata records it as AI-assisted (claude-opus-5-5); an editor's note for the work itself is still to be written.",
